{"id":"neet-2026-q049","pn":49,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Electrode potential from Gibbs energy","q":"The standard electrode potential (E°) for the half-cell reaction Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺ at 298 K is\n(Given : E°(Fe³⁺/Fe) = –0.04 V and E°(Fe²⁺/Fe) = –0.44 V at 298 K)","o":["+0.76 V","–0.48 V","+0.92 V","+0.40 V"],"c":0,"s":"Fe³⁺ → Fe (3 electrons, –0.04 V) is the sum of Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺ (1 electron, E° unknown) and Fe²⁺ → Fe (2 electrons, –0.44 V).\nSo ΔG°(Fe³⁺/Fe) = ΔG°(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) + ΔG°(Fe²⁺/Fe).\nUse ΔG° = –nFE°: –3F(–0.04) = –1F(E°) + (–2F)(–0.44).\n0.12 = –E° + 0.88.\nE° = 0.88 – 0.12 = 0.76 V.","sfig":{"w":240,"h":116,"alt":"Cycle: Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺ with E° marked ?, Fe²⁺ to Fe marked –0.44 V, and Fe³⁺ directly to Fe marked –0.04 V.","d":[["t",30,66,"Fe³⁺"],["l",64,62,104,62,"a"],["t",110,66,"Fe²⁺"],["l",144,62,184,62,"a"],["t",190,66,"Fe"],["p","M42 52 V28 H120 V50","a"],["t",81,22,"?","m"],["p","M134 52 V28 H198 V50","a"],["t",166,22,"–0.44 V","m"],["p","M42 70 V92 H198 V72","a"],["t",120,108,"–0.04 V","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026-q051","pn":51,"y":"NEET 2026 · 21 Jun · Re-exam","tp":"Electrochemistry","st":"Kohlrausch law limiting conductivity","q":"For a salt XY, which is a strong electrolyte, the plot of Λm versus √c has a slope of –90.0 S cm² mol⁻³ᐟ² L¹ᐟ² at 298 K. At 0.01 M concentration of XY, the value of Λm is 145.0 S cm² mol⁻¹. The limiting molar conductivity of Y⁻ ion (λ°Y⁻, in S cm² mol⁻¹) at 298 K will be\n(Given : λ°X⁺ = 74.0 S cm² mol⁻¹)","o":["100.0","90.0","76.0","80.0"],"c":3,"s":"Debye-Huckel-Onsager: Λm = Λ°m – A√c, a straight line with slope –A = –90.\n√0.01 = 0.1, so 145 = Λ°m – 90 × 0.1.\nΛ°m = 154 S cm² mol⁻¹.\nΛ°m(XY) = λ°X⁺ + λ°Y⁻, so 154 = 74 + λ°Y⁻.\nλ°Y⁻ = 80 S cm² mol⁻¹."}
